          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Raphael's Portraits</strong><br>\nRaphael was also renowned for his portraits, which were admired for their clarity, richness in detail, and psychological insight. His portraits of Pope Julius II and other notable figures of the time contributed to his fame and fortune as they conveyed both status and the unique personality of the subjects.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Death</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Raphael ran an extensive workshop and had many students and assistants. His work continued to evolve, displaying greater emotional depth and use of complex color schemes. Raphael died on April 6, 1520, his 37th birthday, leaving behind a legacy as one of the greatest artists of the Renaissance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Raphael's Legacy</strong><br>\nRaphael's influence on art extends beyond his lifetime, with many later artists drawing inspiration from his works. His painting style was imitated by other artists, which helped to spread his style. His artworks continue to be studied for their composition, clarity, and visual storytelling, underlining his status as a master of High Renaissance art.</p>\n"
              },
